<script server>
/**
 * The CSRF token, as a form field.
 *
 * Every form in this application was returning 403.
 *
 * The middleware is double-submit: it compares a value from the request
 * against the `X-CSRF-Token` cookie, and it will take that value from an
 * `x-csrf-token` header **or** from a `_token` body field. A single-page app
 * reads the cookie with JavaScript and echoes the header, which is the path
 * the middleware was written for. This application deliberately runs no
 * client-side JavaScript, so its forms could send neither - and every write
 * from a browser (opening an issue, commenting, creating a label, merging a
 * pull request) failed with `CSRF token mismatch` before it reached an action.
 *
 * So the token goes in the body, which is what a form can carry. One component
 * rather than a line in each form, because a form that forgets it is a button
 * that silently does nothing, and that is exactly the failure nobody notices
 * until somebody tries to use the product.
 *
 * Reading the cookie is enough. The router seeds it on every safe-method
 * response, so by the time a page with a form has been fetched, the browser
 * holds one.
 */
/**
 * The request, when there is one, without naming a binding that may not exist.
 *
 * stx declares `__stxServeContext` on the path that serves a request and on no
 * other, and an *undeclared* identifier is a ReferenceError - which optional
 * chaining does not save, because `x?.y` throws on `x` before the chain is
 * reached. It throws inside the server script's IIFE, so it takes every other
 * variable in this file down with it and the page renders its not-found branch
 * as though the answer were no.
 *
 * The globalThis mirror is not the fix: it is shared between concurrent
 * requests, so reading cookies off it could hand one reader another reader's
 * session.
 */
const serveContext = typeof __stxServeContext === 'undefined' ? undefined : __stxServeContext

const cookies = serveContext?.cookies ?? {}

// Both spellings, because the middleware accepts both and a proxy may
// normalise the case of a cookie name.
const token = String(cookies['X-CSRF-Token'] ?? cookies['x-csrf-token'] ?? cookies['csrf-token'] ?? '')
</script>

<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ token }}">
